ion/id #uuid "501b4a98-609b-5b94-8408-270235db8b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-40756" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,12).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-40756/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection/4cf0eeecc637b43baabc8aa5a3abd2e67a51fbf1e4e31f9ee027fb9c9a03943f" :answer/id #uuid "98c0aacd-a120-5a2c-8cab-44132e1a0161" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection/575ce4b313ad2a97497b301160d3aab753aa993b56af60fdd2dce4c6226a4eb3" :answer/id #uuid "15d30795-7a8b-53ee-aa2b-d3fc70fff641" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection/f128f8b5634493b080ee1f463fc21f4e70f58b294af5db8846f13a37755c6f60" :answer/id #uuid "a9be768a-d6e4-5ae0-9b7e-636ade1860ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection/0e8b3df5c3f6b110764da510fb2cf7a18a10db9756ee691486385f28e0788641" :answer/id #uuid "315bd30f-100f-5493-9845-d4b7586d8c5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection/c9e78409b75e63ff70572f6cfba0904b42065b91976ad6b1346829e093176475" :answer/id #uuid "6bbf35e3-781a-5d5b-9ed7-714c40ca144d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "105a8792-912e-5235-b08e-d4d278812fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40761/selection/0e8b3df5c3f6b110764da510fb2cf7a18a10db9756ee691486385f28e0788641" :answer-field/choices ["q-40761/selection/4cf0eeecc637b43baabc8aa5a3abd2e67a51fbf1e4e31f9ee027fb9c9a03943f" "q-40761/selection/575ce4b313ad2a97497b301160d3aab753aa993b56af60fdd2dce4c6226a4eb3" "q-40761/selection/f128f8b5634493b080ee1f463fc21f4e70f58b294af5db8846f13a37755c6f60" "q-40761/selection/0e8b3df5c3f6b110764da510fb2cf7a18a10db9756ee691486385f28e0788641" "q-40761/selection/c9e78409b75e63ff70572f6cfba0904b42065b91976ad6b1346829e093176475"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40761" :question/id #uuid "ef027f0c-fc1b-5a90-a307-c36c568315d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40761" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-k,k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40761/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-275806/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1f76cb9a-40f1-5c3f-a9d4-4cf44f1d6914" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275806/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "429509a8-b66f-5a5e-ad6d-c517ad9ac724" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-275806/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-275806/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-275806/field-2/09f471dae983db28c0826657524c52e39160889c30208ec0dd3cf10ef9c2407a" :answer/id #uuid "9bedf116-1ecb-5f35-8da3-7700073366f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-275806/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "898b994b-0c53-59f3-94e6-7fea00cc240e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-275806/field-2/09f471dae983db28c0826657524c52e39160889c30208ec0dd3cf10ef9c2407a" :answer-field/choices ["q-275806/field-2/09f471dae983db28c0826657524c52e39160889c30208ec0dd3cf10ef9c2407a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-275806" :question/id #uuid "4ee566b5-e505-5e57-b184-f1dc97ae1c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-275806" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-275806/field-1" "q-275806/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-275806"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
